group: Cs
formula: CH4O
construction: Fragment orbitals (extended Hückel)
headline: 
source: MO levels: RHF, 6-31G* · 
note: Model: order and approximate spacing from extended Hückel (qualitative) · calculation in eV · electrons: C 4 (neutral: 2 unpaired e), drawn 4; H4O 10, drawn 10; model and calculation: 14 drawn + 0 folded (0 levels) = 14 · labels in C<sub>s</sub>\n1 high empty level drawn at the top (not to scale); 4 levels have an overlap below 0.80 with the matched calculated orbital
about: The extended Hückel diagram explains the calculation's orbitals: nothing more, nothing less.\n\n**The model.** Every column is extended Hückel (Alvarez parameters). The middle column is the molecule's own extended Hückel run, the side columns are the two fragments' (C and H4O). The basis is the same in all three, so the lines are an exact projection of each model MO on the fragment orbitals: a line joins a model MO to a fragment level that makes up at least 10 % of it.\n\n**Bonding and antibonding** are read from the sign of each model MO's overlap population between the two fragments: above +0.02 bonding, below -0.02 antibonding (*), otherwise non-bonding. A bonding and an antibonding level of the same degeneracy that are the in-phase and out-of-phase combinations of the same fragment orbitals are joined as partners and share a colour; a level without such a partner keeps its own classification.\n\n**Scale.** The model columns show the order of the levels and their approximate spacing from extended Hückel, not a scale: close levels are drawn further apart so they can be read, and no model energy is printed (it is in the hover text). Extended Hückel antibonding energies are qualitative and typically far too high for short bonds to diffuse atoms such as B, Be and Al.\n\n**The calculation** is the column on the right, on its own energy axis in eV (not on the model's scale). For each model level it shows the calculated orbital (or set of near-degenerate orbitals) with the largest overlap with it, at its calculated energy, in the colour of the model level it matches. Beside each matched level, "overlap" is |<model MO|calculated MO>| (a number from 0 to 1); values below 0.80 are counted in the note under the diagram. Labels are those of the matched calculated orbital. Clicking a level shows that orbital in 3-D.\n\nThe model explains the calculated occupied orbitals in the right order. The order of some empty levels above the LUMO differs between the model and the calculation. Virtual orbitals above the LUMO depend strongly on the basis set.\n\n**Total electrons: 14** (C 4, H4O 10).\n\n**Construction labels.** sigma, pi and delta are taken relative to each bond from the centre to a ligand atom: sigma = no nodal plane containing the bond axis, pi = one, delta = two (s and a p orbital along the bond have none, a p orbital across it one, a d orbital as many as its m about the bond). For a level made of several ligand orbitals the share on each type is averaged over the bonds, and the label is the dominant one; the hover text gives the split. * marks the out-of-phase combination of the leading fragment orbitals, nb a level with under 5 % on the other fragment. For a diatomic the levels of each sigma/pi, g/u class are named in the textbook order (2s before 2p, bonding before antibonding) whatever their s/p mixing.\n\n**Effect on the bond.** ECCE lengthens the bond slightly and checks whether the orbital's energy goes up or down. If it goes up, the orbital strengthens the bond; if it goes down, it weakens it. The hover text gives the change as dε/dR; under 0.5 eV/Å is called little effect. This is a model estimate: for orbitals with only a small effect it can point the wrong way (N₂ σ2p, for example).\n\nEach model level carries a short grey tag naming the fragment orbitals it is mostly made of (shares of at least 20 %), such as Cr d or CO π*. Ligand-field view hides empty ligand-centred levels such as CO π*; transitions into them are charge transfer, not d–d.\n\n**Caveats.** 1 high empty level drawn at the top (not to scale); 4 levels have an overlap below 0.80 with the matched calculated orbital. Extended Hückel puts empty antibonding orbitals tens of eV up, so an empty level more than 30 eV above the HOMO is drawn at the top of the diagram, marked with an up arrow, or not at all when it sits almost entirely on one fragment. A loose match means no calculated orbital resembles the model level closely; read its calculated energy with care.\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in Cs; the code reported none.\n\n**Picked atoms.** One atom is drawn against the atoms bonded to it; two bonded atoms cut that bond and the two sides are drawn against each other (in and out of phase when they are equal). A metal gives the fragment-orbital diagram, each ligand being a connected piece left when the metal is removed. A single atom that is not a metal is drawn the same way when it is not fixed by every symmetry operation of the molecule, or when atoms are bonded to neither it nor its neighbours: the atom against the connected pieces left when it is removed, each piece neutral.
verdict: differs The model explains the calculated occupied orbitals in the right order. The empty levels differ: the calculated LUMO is 8a', the model's is 11a'*.
